    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_c059bf7a362144145937d9d732eae2a2af96c11f1d9722eaafb199522eec7fe6",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The corrected mean supplies a rigorous but strictly weaker positivity signal for the motivating zero-count criterion: for each fixed 0<σ<1 it forces infinitely many sampled values Re ζ(σ+i g_n)>0, while the argument-principle reduction requires Re ζ>0 at every point of an entire horizontal boundary segment at a Gram height to make the zero count a nearest-integer value.",
